acted by reading from the\0A * resulting operation to produce keys and other cryptographic material.\0A *\0A * If this function returns an error status, the operation enters an error\0A * state and must be aborted by calling psa_key_derivation_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation       The key derivation operation object to use.\0A *                                It must have been set up with\0A *                                psa_key_derivation_setup() with a\0A *                                key agreement and derivation algorithm\0A *                                \5Cc alg (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                                #PSA_ALG_IS_KEY_AGREEMENT(\5Cc alg) is true\0A *                                and #PSA_ALG_IS_RAW_KEY_AGREEMENT(\5Cc alg)\0A *                                is false).\0A *                                The operation must be ready for an\0A *                                input of the type given by \5Cp step.\0A * \5Cparam step                    Which step the input data is for.\0A * \5Cparam private_key             Identifier of the private key to use. It must\0A *                                allow the usage #PSA_KEY_USAGE_DERIVE.\0A * \5Cparam[in] peer_key      Public key of the peer. The peer key must be in the\0A *                          same format that psa_import_key() accepts for the\0A *                          public key type corresponding to the type of\0A *                          private_key. That is, this function performs the\0A *                          equivalent of\0A *                          #psa_import_key(...,\0A *                          `peer_key`, `peer_key_length`) where\0A *                          with key attributes indicating the public key\0A *                          type corresponding to the type of `private_key`.\0A *                          For example, for EC keys, this means that peer_key\0A *                          is interpreted as a point on the curve that the\0A *                          private key is on. The standard formats for public\0A *                          keys are documented in the documentation of\0A *                          psa_export_public_key().\0A * \5Cparam peer_key_length         Size of \5Cp peer_key in bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cc private_key is not compatible with \5Cc alg,\0A *         or \5Cp peer_key is not valid for \5Cc alg or not compatible with\0A *         \5Cc private_key, or \5Cc step does not allow an input resulting\0A *         from a key agreement.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cc alg is not supported or is not a key derivation algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid for this key agreement \5Cp step,\0A *         or the library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_key_derivation_key_agreement(\0A    psa_key_derivation_operation_t *operation,\0A    psa_key_derivation_step_t step,\0A    mbedtls_svc_key_id_t private_key,\0A    const uint8_t *peer_key,\0A    size_t peer_key_length);\0A\0A/** Read some data from a key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * This function calculates output bytes from a key derivation algorithm and\0A * return those bytes.\0A * If you view the key derivation's output as a stream of bytes, this\0A * function destructively reads the requested number of bytes from the\0A * stream.\0A * The operation's capacity decreases by the number of bytes read.\0A *\0A * If this function returns an error status other than\0A * #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_DATA, the operation enters an error\0A * state and must be aborted by calling psa_key_derivation_abort().\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation The key derivation operation object to read from.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output       Buffer where the output will be written.\0A * \5Cparam output_length     Number of bytes to output.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED\0A *         One of the inputs was a key whose policy didn't allow\0A *         #PSA_KEY_USAGE_DERIVE.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_DATA\0A *                          The operation's capacity was less than\0A *                          \5Cp output_length bytes. Note that in this case,\0A *                          no output is written to the output buffer.\0A *                          The operation's capacity is set to 0, thus\0A *                          subsequent calls to this function will not\0A *                          succeed, even with a smaller output buffer.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be active and completed\0A *         all required input steps), or the library has not been previously\0A *         initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_key_derivation_output_bytes(\0A    psa_key_derivation_operation_t *operation,\0A    uint8_t *output,\0A    size_t output_length);\0A\0A/** Derive a key from an ongoing key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * This function calculates output bytes from a key derivation algorithm\0A * and uses those bytes to generate a key deterministically.\0A * The key's location, usage policy, type and size are taken from\0A * \5Cp attributes.\0A *\0A * If you view the key derivation's output as a stream of bytes, this\0A * function destructively reads as many bytes as required from the\0A * stream.\0A * The operation's capacity decreases by the number of bytes read.\0A *\0A * If this function returns an error status other than\0A * #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_DATA, the operation enters an error\0A * state and must be aborted by calling psa_key_derivation_abort().\0A *\0A * How much output is produced and consumed from the operation, and how\0A * the key is derived, depends on the key type and on the key size\0A * (denoted \5Cc bits below):\0A *\0A * - For key types for which the key is an arbitrary sequence of bytes\0A *   of a given size, this function is functionally equivalent to\0A *   calling #psa_key_derivation_output_bytes\0A *   and passing the resulting output to #psa_import_key.\0A *   However, this function has a security benefit:\0A *   if the implementation provides an isolation boundary then\0A *   the key material is not exposed outside the isolation boundary.\0A *   As a consequence, for these key types, this function always consumes\0A *   exactly (\5Cc bits / 8) bytes from the operation.\0A *   The following key types defined in this specification follow this scheme:\0A *\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_AES;\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_ARIA;\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_CAMELLIA;\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_DERIVE;\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_HMAC;\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_PASSWORD_HASH.\0A *\0A * - For ECC keys on a Montgomery elliptic curve\0A *   (#PSA_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR(\5Cc curve) where \5Cc curve designates a\0A *   Montgomery curve), this function always draws a byte string whose\0A *   length is determined by the curve, and sets the mandatory bits\0A *   accordingly. That is:\0A *\0A *     - Curve25519 (#PSA_ECC_FAMILY_MONTGOMERY, 255 bits): draw a 32-byte\0A *       string and process it as specified in RFC 7748 &sect;5.\0A *     - Curve448 (#PSA_ECC_FAMILY_MONTGOMERY, 448 bits): draw a 56-byte\0A *       string and process it as specified in RFC 7748 &sect;5.\0A *\0A * - For key types for which the key is represented by a single sequence of\0A *   \5Cc bits bits with constraints as to which bit sequences are acceptable,\0A *   this function draws a byte string of length (\5Cc bits / 8) bytes rounded\0A *   up to the nearest whole number of bytes. If the resulting byte string\0A *   is acceptable, it becomes the key, otherwise the drawn bytes are discarded.\0A *   This process is repeated until an acceptable byte string is drawn.\0A *   The byte string drawn from the operation is interpreted as specified\0A *   for the output produced by psa_export_key().\0A *   The following key types defined in this specification follow this scheme:\0A *\0A *     - #PSA_KEY_TYPE_DES.\0A *       Force-set the parity bits, but discard forbidden weak keys.\0A *       For 2-key and 3-key triple-DES, the three keys are generated\0A *       successively (for example, for 3-key triple-DES,\0A *       if the first 8 bytes specify a weak key and the next 8 bytes do not,\0A *       discard the first 8 bytes, use the next 8 bytes as the first key,\0A *       and continue reading output from the operation to derive the other\0A *       two keys).\0A *     - Finite-field Diffie-Hellman keys (#PSA_KEY_TYPE_DH_KEY_PAIR(\5Cc group)\0A *       where \5Cc group designates any Diffie-Hellman group) and\0A *       ECC keys on a Weierstrass elliptic curve\0A *       (#PSA_KEY_TYPE_ECC_KEY_PAIR(\5Cc curve) where \5Cc curve designates a\0A *       Weierstrass curve).\0A *       For these key types, interpret the